  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Enthalpy is defined at constant pressure, which is why an open beaker calorimeter measures ΔH.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Heat is released to the surroundings, so the products have less enthalpy than the reactants and ΔH is negative.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A negative ΔH means heat is given out, so the reaction is exothermic.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bond enthalpy is the energy needed to break one mole of a given bond in the gaseous state.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two moles of water are formed, so the value per mole is −572 ÷ 2 = −286 kJ mol⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Condensation releases the latent heat of vaporisation, so it warms the surroundings.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Energy must be absorbed, so the products sit higher on the enthalpy diagram.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Combustion is complete reaction with oxygen, with all reactants and products in their standard states.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There is no change when an element forms itself, so the value is defined as zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Enthalpy is a state function, so the overall change is the same whichever path is taken.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Formation means one mole of the compound made from its elements in their standard states.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A positive sign means heat is taken in from the surroundings.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A sealed steel bomb keeps the volume constant, so the heat measured is ΔU, close to ΔH for these reactions.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Energy is quoted per mole of bonds, so 0.50 mol needs 218 kJ.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bonds broken = 436 + 243 = 679; bonds made = 2 × 431 = 862; ΔH = 679 − 862 = −183 kJ mol⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Forming a bond releases energy; breaking a bond absorbs it.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    q = mcΔT = 100 × 4.2 × 40 = 16 800 J = 16.8 kJ.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The plastic cup is a good insulator and simple calorimeter for reactions in solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Heat leaks to the air and the cup, so the measured temperature rise is too small.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    0.25 × 94 = 23.5 kJ, released because the sign is negative.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Products = 3(−394) + 4(−286) = −2326; reactants = −104; ΔH = −2326 − (−104) = −2222 kJ mol⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A large increase in entropy, as when a gas is produced, can drive an endothermic change as well.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Burning methane releases heat; the others need energy supplied.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is essentially the enthalpy change of H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O, which is close to −57 kJ mol⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The products lie lower in energy, so the arrow points down and ΔH is negative.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Particles need at least the activation energy for a collision to lead to reaction.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The solution takes heat from its surroundings, so the temperature falls and ΔH is positive.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Specific heat capacity is the energy per gram per kelvin rise.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2.85 ÷ 0.050 = 57 kJ mol⁻¹, released, so −57 kJ mol⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    q = mcΔT needs all three, and dividing by the moles gives the molar value.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    q = 100 × 4.2 × 30 = 12 600 J = 12.6 kJ.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Few particles have enough energy to react, so the reaction proceeds slowly.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It provides an alternative route with a lower energy barrier; ΔH is unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hess's law adds the steps: +20 + (−65) = −45 kJ.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    ΔH = total bond energy broken − total bond energy formed.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    If the hydration energy released exceeds the lattice energy needed, dissolving is exothermic.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Standard conditions are 298 K and 1 atm with substances in their standard states; a catalyst is irrelevant.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two moles of CO are formed, so per mole the value is −111 kJ mol⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    q = 50 × 4.2 × 6.7 = 1407 J; 0.050 mol reacts, so 1407 ÷ 0.050 = 28.1 kJ mol⁻¹ is released, giving −28 kJ mol⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
